["<a class=\"card\" data-card data-cat=\"ai\"\n data-hay=\"Cerebras hits 4400 tok/s on a single chip: inference speed is the moat Cerebras pushes WSE 3.5 to 4400 tok/s per chip, and the CS4 rack packs three wafers into a 120kW cabinet. The core argument: inference has to be split into prefill and decode, and speed is the moat. TechTechPotato TechTechPotato GPT-5.6 Sol Ultrafast.... Just Got UltraFaster (Cerebras 4th Gen) Inference chips Cerebras Wafer-scale computing SRAM OpenAI Compute Wafer Scale Engine (WSE) Cross-reticle stitching Backpack Prefill/Decode Parametric yield\" href=\"/podcast/en/p/2026-08-19-techtechpotato-推理速度才是新護城河-cerebras-機架化再提速/\">\n<div class=\"cover\"><img src=\"https://i2.ytimg.com/vi/ezbLa-c2MBM/hqdefault.jpg\" alt=\"\" loading=\"lazy\" decoding=\"async\" data-initial=\"T\"><span class=\"dur\">23:20</span></div>\n<div class=\"card-body\">\n<div class=\"kicker\" data-cat=\"ai\"><span class=\"src\">TechTechPotato</span>\n<span class=\"date\">0819</span></div>\n<h2>Cerebras hits 4400 tok/s on a single chip: inference speed is the moat</h2>\n<p class=\"dek\">Cerebras pushes WSE 3.5 to 4400 tok/s per chip, and the CS4 rack packs three wafers into a 120kW cabinet. The core argument: inference has to be split into prefill and decode, and speed is the moat.</p>\n<div class=\"card-foot\">\n<span class=\"badge\"><b>8</b> Points</span>\n<span class=\"badge\"><b>7</b> Quotes</span>\n<span class=\"tag\">Inference chips</span><span class=\"tag\">Cerebras</span>\n</div></div></a>", "<a class=\"card\" data-card data-cat=\"hist\"\n data-hay=\"Thirty Thousand Canadians Moved: The Young Were Saved, the Old Lost Everything From 1954, nearly thirty thousand people in Newfoundland, Canada, were moved from remote fishing villages to towns. The younger generation gained education and a way out; the old and the Inuit lost their homes, scattered until there was no way left to reach one another. Resettlement has no single verdict. Witness History Witness History Canada&#x27;s controversial resettlement programme Urbanization Resettlement Newfoundland Oral history Regional policy Community collapse outport growth centre Confederation\" href=\"/podcast/en/p/2026-08-19-witness-加拿大三萬人大搬遷-最好的事與最糟的事/\">\n<div class=\"cover\"><img src=\"https://ichef.bbci.co.uk/images/ic/400x400/p0kt82ds.jpg\" alt=\"\" loading=\"lazy\" decoding=\"async\" data-initial=\"W\"><span class=\"dur\">9:15</span></div>\n<div class=\"card-body\">\n<div class=\"kicker\" data-cat=\"hist\"><span class=\"src\">Witness History</span>\n<span class=\"date\">0819</span></div>\n<h2>Thirty Thousand Canadians Moved: The Young Were Saved, the Old Lost Everything</h2>\n<p class=\"dek\">From 1954, nearly thirty thousand people in Newfoundland, Canada, were moved from remote fishing villages to towns. The younger generation gained education and a way out; the old and the Inuit lost their homes, scattered until there was no way left to reach one another. Resettlement has no single verdict.</p>\n<div class=\"card-foot\">\n<span class=\"badge\"><b>7</b> Points</span>\n<span class=\"badge\"><b>8</b> Quotes</span>\n<span class=\"tag\">Urbanization</span><span class=\"tag\">Resettlement</span>\n</div></div></a>", "<a class=\"card\" data-card data-cat=\"ideas\"\n data-hay=\"Keto reversed his son&#x27;s bipolar; then Roblox&#x27;s founder split decisions into four layers David Baszucki lays out Roblox&#x27;s decision-making operating system in four layers: the body&#x27;s metabolic state governs major judgment calls, long-term perspective multiplied by fast iteration, general systems beat hard-coding, and when an old model stops working you replace the entire economic system rather than patch it. The Knowledge Project The Knowledge Project Make Better Decisions by Fixing Yourself First Decision-making Org management Keto Roblox Founder playbook Virtual economies CGM (continuous glucose monitor) Robux (Roblox&#x27;s virtual currency) MCP (Model Context Protocol) Lua (the Lua programming language)\" href=\"/podcast/en/p/2026-08-18-knowledge-先修代謝再談決策-roblox-創始人反常識管理/\">\n<div class=\"cover\"><img src=\"https://megaphone.imgix.net/podcasts/7d43192a-94ce-11f1-9294-d7cbd16ea1bb/image/6365e2150857f4234eab35839aa0b50d.png?ixlib=rails-4.3.1&amp;max-w=3000&amp;max-h=3000&amp;fit=crop&amp;auto=format,compress&amp;w=400&amp;auto=format\" alt=\"\" loading=\"lazy\" decoding=\"async\" data-initial=\"T\"><span class=\"dur\">46:33</span></div>\n<div class=\"card-body\">\n<div class=\"kicker\" data-cat=\"ideas\"><span class=\"src\">The Knowledge Project</span>\n<span class=\"date\">0818</span></div>\n<h2>Keto reversed his son&#x27;s bipolar; then Roblox&#x27;s founder split decisions into four layers</h2>\n<p class=\"dek\">David Baszucki lays out Roblox&#x27;s decision-making operating system in four layers: the body&#x27;s metabolic state governs major judgment calls, long-term perspective multiplied by fast iteration, general systems beat hard-coding, and when an old model stops working you replace the entire economic system rather than patch it.</p>\n<div class=\"card-foot\">\n<span class=\"badge\"><b>8</b> Points</span>\n<span class=\"badge\"><b>7</b> Quotes</span>\n<span class=\"tag\">Decision-making</span><span class=\"tag\">Org management</span>\n</div></div></a>", "<a class=\"card\" data-card data-cat=\"parent\"\n data-hay=\"Custody in divorce: courts now default to 50/50, and the parent playing to win loses A New York divorce lawyer maps a decade of drift: courts have moved from ‘prove why it shouldn&#x27;t be sole custody’ to ‘why not 50/50’ — and what actually determines how the child comes out is not the schedule but whether the parents can put the child&#x27;s needs ahead of their own pain. Securely Attached Securely Attached Child custody through divorce: Navigating the courts and building a custody schedule that works with Michael Fried Divorce Child custody Joint custody US family law Co-parenting Parent-child bond OurFamilyWizard collaborative law parent coordinator change of circumstances forensic psychologist best interest of the child\" href=\"/podcast/en/p/2026-08-18-secureattach-法院已默認五五開-贏家心態才是真正的輸/\">\n<div class=\"cover\"><img src=\"https://static.libsyn.com/p/assets/4/0/e/9/40e91292d34d26f7e55e3c100dce7605/Design_1_Final.png\" alt=\"\" loading=\"lazy\" decoding=\"async\" data-initial=\"S\"><span class=\"dur\">52:28</span></div>\n<div class=\"card-body\">\n<div class=\"kicker\" data-cat=\"parent\"><span class=\"src\">Securely Attached</span>\n<span class=\"date\">0818</span></div>\n<h2>Custody in divorce: courts now default to 50/50, and the parent playing to win loses</h2>\n<p class=\"dek\">A New York divorce lawyer maps a decade of drift: courts have moved from ‘prove why it shouldn&#x27;t be sole custody’ to ‘why not 50/50’ — and what actually determines how the child comes out is not the schedule but whether the parents can put the child&#x27;s needs ahead of their own pain.</p>\n<div class=\"card-foot\">\n<span class=\"badge\"><b>8</b> Points</span>\n<span class=\"badge\"><b>6</b> Quotes</span>\n<span class=\"tag\">Divorce</span><span class=\"tag\">Child custody</span>\n</div></div></a>", "<a class=\"card\" data-card data-cat=\"parent\"\n data-hay=\"You Can&#x27;t Make a Kid Want to Work: Structure and Consequences Beat Enthusiasm Building a work ethic doesn&#x27;t run on making a kid enjoy the work. It runs on three things: rules that structure the household, privileges tied to responsibilities, and the sense that someone is counting on him — plus, when needed, letting him take his own fall at school. Ask Lisa: The Psychology of Raising Tweens &amp; Teens Ask Lisa 285: ENCORE: How Do I Help My Kid Develop a Work Ethic? Parenting Teen Psychology Intrinsic Motivation Responsibility Family Structure work ethic intrinsic vs extrinsic motivation warmth and structure complain but comply purpose nagging\" href=\"/podcast/en/p/2026-08-18-asklisa-孩子不愛幹活不用治-結構與後果比熱愛更有效/\">\n<div class=\"cover\"><img src=\"https://megaphone.imgix.net/podcasts/c55e4104-cbbe-11ec-93d2-ab245e93324f/image/478fcfa676f260bc9a5628875ab7e0af.png?ixlib=rails-4.3.1&amp;max-w=3000&amp;max-h=3000&amp;fit=crop&amp;auto=format,compress&amp;w=400&amp;auto=format\" alt=\"\" loading=\"lazy\" decoding=\"async\" data-initial=\"A\"><span class=\"dur\">32:45</span></div>\n<div class=\"card-body\">\n<div class=\"kicker\" data-cat=\"parent\"><span class=\"src\">Ask Lisa: The Psychology of Raising Tweens &amp; Teens</span>\n<span class=\"date\">0818</span></div>\n<h2>You Can&#x27;t Make a Kid Want to Work: Structure and Consequences Beat Enthusiasm</h2>\n<p class=\"dek\">Building a work ethic doesn&#x27;t run on making a kid enjoy the work. It runs on three things: rules that structure the household, privileges tied to responsibilities, and the sense that someone is counting on him — plus, when needed, letting him take his own fall at school.</p>\n<div class=\"card-foot\">\n<span class=\"badge\"><b>8</b> Points</span>\n<span class=\"badge\"><b>7</b> Quotes</span>\n<span class=\"tag\">Parenting</span><span class=\"tag\">Teen Psychology</span>\n</div></div></a>", "<a class=\"card\" data-card data-cat=\"hist\"\n data-hay=\"Mandalay hinged on one call: drop the frontal assault, lay siege In March 1945 British forces attacked Mandalay in Burma; against Fort Dufferin&#x27;s thick walls and deep moat, the frontal assault failed. The division commander refused to keep storming it and switched to a siege, and the Japanese eventually abandoned the fort. Witness History Witness History The battle of Mandalay in WW2 WWII Burma Mandalay Siege warfare BBC field recordings Commonwealth forces Fort Dufferin 19th Indian Division Frontier Force Regiment 14th Army\" href=\"/podcast/en/p/2026-08-18-witness-曼德勒戰役勝負手-放棄正面突擊改用圍困/\">\n<div class=\"cover\"><img src=\"https://ichef.bbci.co.uk/images/ic/400x400/p0kt82ds.jpg\" alt=\"\" loading=\"lazy\" decoding=\"async\" data-initial=\"W\"><span class=\"dur\">10:59</span></div>\n<div class=\"card-body\">\n<div class=\"kicker\" data-cat=\"hist\"><span class=\"src\">Witness History</span>\n<span class=\"date\">0818</span></div>\n<h2>Mandalay hinged on one call: drop the frontal assault, lay siege</h2>\n<p class=\"dek\">In March 1945 British forces attacked Mandalay in Burma; against Fort Dufferin&#x27;s thick walls and deep moat, the frontal assault failed. The division commander refused to keep storming it and switched to a siege, and the Japanese eventually abandoned the fort.</p>\n<div class=\"card-foot\">\n<span class=\"badge\"><b>7</b> Points</span>\n<span class=\"badge\"><b>6</b> Quotes</span>\n<span class=\"tag\">WWII</span><span class=\"tag\">Burma</span>\n</div></div></a>"]